{"uuid":"42c7d7f8-6793-4215-b973-24571a24cc67","parentUuid":"96a2787b-b577-4365-a216-57de69c1f884","sessionId":"8a011258-1951-4d2e-8cd6-380364f29e1c","timestamp":"2026-10-18T04:02:28.843Z","type":"assistant","provenance":"assistant_output","cwd":"work/weak/qwen/rally-3d-sun-0","version":"0.25.1","gitBranch":"master","model":"bench-model","message":{"role":"model","parts":[{"text":"I need to implement the sunPosition function using the SunCalc formulas. Let me recall the standard astronomical calculations:\n\n1. Calculate the day of year from the date\n2. Calculate the mean anomaly (M)\n3. Calculate the equation of center (C)\n4. Calculate the ecliptic longitude (λ)\n5. Calculate the sun's declination (δ)\n6. Calculate the right ascension (α)\n7. Calculate the hour angle (H)\n8. Calculate altitude (alt) and bearing from these\n\nThe formulas are:\n- Day of year: based on the date\n- Mean anomaly: M = 2π * (n - 1) / 365, where n is day of year\n- Equation of center: C ≈ 1.915 * sin(M) - 0.02 * sin(2M) + 0.0003 * sin(3M) (in degrees)\n- Ecliptic longitude: λ = L0 + M + C, where L0 is the longitude of the sun at the vernal equinox (~282.66°)\n- Declination: δ = arcsin(sin(ε) * sin(λ)), where ε is the obliquity (~23.44°)\n- Right ascension: α = atan2(cos(ε) * sin(λ) * tan(δ) + cos(λ), cos(λ))\n- Hour angle: H = local solar time - 12 hours (converted to radians)\n- Altitude: sin(alt) = sin(lat) * sin(δ) + cos(lat) * cos(δ) * cos(H)\n- Bearing: from the hour angle and declination\n\nLet me implement this carefully.\n","thought":true},{"text":"\n\nNow let me implement the `sunPosition` function using the SunCalc formulas:\n\n"},{"functionCall":{"id":"call_5C9D5328E03C4BD9AA230A08","name":"write_file","args":{"content":"// Sun position (the SunCalc formulas). Not implemented yet.\n\nexport function sunPosition(date, lat, lng) {\n  throw new Error('sunPosition is not implemented');\n}\n","file_path":"work/weak/qwen/rally-3d-sun-0/src/sun.js"}}}]},"usageMetadata":{"promptToken